# Lesson 3, new exercise: give up a diamond early so that dummy can ruff the third one.
title: Give a diamond, ruff a diamond
id: L03-21
type: exercise
lesson: 3
concepts: 3.3, 3.4
student: S
board: 47
dealer: S
vulnerable: NS

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  Q832   864    53     KQ84
  E  76     T732   T972   AJ9
  S  AKJ94  AK5    A64    72
  W  T5     QJ9    KQJ8   T653

auction:
  S 1S*    = 13+ points, at least four spades
     not *: 19 high-card points and five spades. Too many for 1NT, too few for a two-bid: what's left?
  W Pass
  N 2S     = 6-9 points and spade support
  E Pass
  S 4S*    = Game
     key: 20 points with the doubleton, plus at least 6 from partner. That's 26. Bid game.
     not Pass: Did you add it up? Partner has at least 6, and 20 plus 6 is 26.
     not 3S: Can your side have less than 25? 20 plus at least 6 is already 26.
     not *: Partner showed 6-9 and spade support. Can you see 25 points?
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1♠ and partner raised to 2♠. With 20 points, you knew your side
  had at least 26, so you bid game. You are declarer, because you bid spades
  first, and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 4
  losers: 3
  spades: 0 = Solid.
  hearts: 1 = The third heart.
  diamonds: 1 = Two small ones. Give up one, and dummy can ruff the other.
  clubs: 1 = The ace.
  remind: You can afford three losers in 4♠. Count the obvious losers suit by suit in your own hand.
  remind: Dummy has only two diamonds. What has to happen before it can ruff your third one?
play:
  1   W DK  N D3  E D2  S DA
      note: Four losers: one heart, two diamonds and the ♣A. Dummy has just two diamonds, so after one more round it can ruff your third diamond. Draw trumps first: dummy will still have trumps left.
  2   S S4  W S5  N SQ  E S6
      note: Draw trumps. Start with dummy's queen, the high card from the short side, so that you finish in your own hand.
  3   N S2  E S7  S SA  W ST
      note: Both opponents followed twice. Trumps are drawn, and dummy still has two.
  4   S D4  W DJ  N D5  E D7
      note: Now give up a diamond. After this, dummy has none left.
  5   W HQ  N H4  E H2  S HA
  6   S D6  W DQ  N S8  E D9
      note: Ruff your last diamond in dummy.
  7   N CK  E CA  S C2  W C3
      note: Now force out the ♣A. Dummy's other clubs will be winners.
  8   E H3  S HK  W HJ  N H6
  9   S C7  W C5  N CQ  E C9
      note: Over to dummy's ♣Q.
  10  N C8  E CJ  S S9  W C6
      note: East still has the ♣J, so ruff.
  11  S H5  W H9  N H8  E HT
      note: Your last heart is the loser you counted.
  12  E H7  S SJ  W D8  N C4
  13  S SK  W CT  N S3  E DT
      note: 10 tricks: 4♠ made.

play recap:
  The plan: count four losers, draw trumps, give up a diamond, and ruff
  your third diamond in dummy.

  Giving up the second diamond early was the key. It cost nothing, since
  that diamond was always a loser, and it made dummy short enough to ruff.
